  "configuration_id": "config_011",
  "reason": "Evidence: RNA (scgpt, singleprofile) is the dominant signal (acc 0.411, F1 0.378), image alone is weak (0.208), and multimodal weighted fusion gives only a marginal gain over RNA alone (0.421 vs 0.411 acc; 0.412 vs 0.378 F1). The profile setting (single vs multi) is a completely unexplored dimension that could substantially shift the performance landscape. Question: Does switching from singleprofile to multiprofile with avgpool aggregation improve the RNA representation? This experiment (config_011: rna_only, multiprofile, scgpt, avgpool) is a clean, single-variable comparison against config_038 (rna_only, singleprofile, scgpt, no aggregation), isolating the multiprofile+aggregation effect on the dominant modality. Why this is best under budget: If multiprofile helps RNA, it justifies investing further experiments in multiprofile multimodal configurations; if it does not, we can confidently restrict the search to singleprofile and focus remaining budget on RNA encoder choice or image encoder quality. Testing this in the RNA-only setting (rather than multimodal) avoids confounding the profile effect with fusion/aggregation interactions, giving the clearest signal about one of the largest unexplored design axes."
}

